\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_kstar}
\label{sec:one_wash}
\label{sec:negative_graduated}
\label{sec:repr_dependence}
\label{sec:boundary_cases}
\label{sec:length_confound}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jsd_decay.pdf}
\caption{Token-level JSD between adjacent depth pairs on log scale. Blue band: min-max range across 8 C4 models (1B--14B) with $\Kempirical{=}2$; dashed purple: Mistral-7B ($\Kempirical{=}1$, cross-scorer validated); green: arXiv Pythia ($\Kempirical{=}3$); dotted red: retrain-chain ($\Kempirical{=}7$). C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff ($\lambda \approx 2.2$--$3.5$), while arXiv decays gradually ($\lambda \approx 1.0$) and retrain-chain shows no cliff ($\lambda = 0.285$).}
\label{fig:jsd_decay}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{One-wash laundering (web text).}
For general web text, $\Kempirical{=}2$ reflects concentrated information loss: 0v1 is near-trivially separable ($\auc \geq 0.92$), 1v2 drops by ${\sim}$30\,pp, and 2v3 falls below $\theta$, so a single generation pass renders text indistinguishable from deeper content.
Neither frontier LLMs nor alternative classifiers improve depth recovery beyond this boundary (McNemar $p > 0.05$; Appendix).

\paragraph{JSD decay and the discrimination boundary.}
Token-level JSD between adjacent generations reveals two decay regimes (Figure~\ref{fig:jsd_decay}): C4 models exhibit a sharp cliff (0v1 JSD exceeds 1v2 by 6--56$\times$) followed by rapid convergence, while arXiv decays ${\sim}$3$\times$ slower ($\lambda \approx 1.0$ vs.\ $2.9$).
The retrain-chain ($\lambda = 0.285$, no cliff) reflects cumulative model-adaptation artifacts, extending $\Kempirical$ to 7 (Appendix~\ref{sec:jsd_extended}).

\paragraph{Graduated filtering failure.}
For web text, 2v3 $\auc < \theta$ across all configurations, so depth assignments beyond $d{=}2$ approximate random labels; the strategy ordering binary $>$ graduated aligns with $\Kempirical{=}2$ (on arXiv, graduated filtering similarly shows no improvement; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).

\paragraph{Representation and scorer dependence.}
$\Kempirical$ is representation-dependent (frozen Qwen embeddings yield $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$ vs.\ $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ for Pythia and OLMo; Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}) and scorer-dependent (cross-family scoring reduces Wikipedia $\Kempirical$ from 2 to 1, Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}, while preserving C4 feature rankings $\rho > 0.90$ and $\Kempirical$ for 6/8 configurations, Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).

\paragraph{Domain dependence of $\Kempirical$.}
C4 yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (8/9 configurations), Python code $\Kempirical{=}2$ for three models ($\Kempirical{=}1$ for capacity-limited Pythia-1.4B; Section~\ref{sec:cross_domain}), arXiv $\Kempirical{=}3$ (five models, four families), and Wikipedia $\Kempirical{=}1$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}); the ordering correlates with structural complexity (Table~\ref{tab:domain-ttr-entropy}).
Code and C4 share $\Kempirical{=}2$ but with shifted cliff positions: syntactic constraints preserve depth-1 signal in code, so domain structure governs both $\Kempirical$ and the decay profile.
The only C4 exception (Mistral-7B, $\Kempirical{=}1$) reaches $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv; a LaTeX ablation confirms content rather than formatting drives this difference (Appendix~\ref{sec:latex_ablation}).

\paragraph{Surprisal attenuation and boundary variation.}
\label{sec:surprisal_attenuation}
The Mistral-7B exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$) arises from compressed surprisal dynamic range (Table~\ref{tab:cohens_d_full}); larger 0$\to$1 surprisal drops correlate with higher 1v2 $\auc$ (Table~\ref{tab:borderline_features}), predicting $\Kempirical$ may decrease beyond 14B.

